Abstract

Medical image fusion plays a vital role in modern healthcare by integrating complementary diagnostic details from multiple imaging modalities, such as Computed Tomography (CT) and Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI), into a unified composite representation. This integrated visualization is essential for clinical decision-making across neurology, oncology, and surgical planning. Although deep learning frameworks have significantly advanced image synthesis, conventional approaches relying on off-the-shelf pre-trained convolutional networks often suffer from feature domain mismatch, as these backbones are primarily optimized on natural photographic images rather than radiological scans. Furthermore, emerging vision transformer architectures impose severe computational overheads, limiting their practical deployment on resource-constrained clinical edge devices. To address these challenges, this study presents a lightweight, parameter-efficient framework based on a selectively fine-tuned VGG19 architecture integrated with a Dual-Attention Siamese Network (DASN). By freezing early convolutional layers and optimizing high-level semantic blocks, the network restricts trainable parameters to just 2.48 M while capturing rich modality-specific anatomical cues. A dual-attention gating mechanism dynamically routes dense cortical bone structures from CT and high-contrast soft-tissue parenchyma from MRI into a refined composite without manual thresholding. The proposed model is trained and evaluated on 184 registered axial brain scan pairs from the benchmark Harvard Medical Image Fusion Dataset. Comprehensive experimental results demonstrate that the proposed DASN framework substantially outperforms baseline and contemporary deep learning models, achieving a Spatial Frequency of 33.89 (a 106.35% improvement), an Average Gradient of 53.24 (an 85.82% improvement), an Entropy of 4.45 (a 16.37% improvement), and a Structural Similarity Index of 0.7158, alongside an ultra-low inference latency of approximately 38 ms per slice for real-time clinical applications.

---

**Keywords:** Medical Image Fusion, Lightweight Deep Learning, Dual-Attention Network, Fine-Tuned VGG19, Harvard Medical Image Fusion Dataset, CT-MRI Synthesis, Edge AI.
